New ticket, STOI-14841 — azure-cli on the RHEL Azure golden images. Set up a fresh ticket tree + branch off main per our standard workflow (slug it something like azure-cli-rhel). Investigate and confirm first, then implement on the branch — but do NOT commit, push, or raise any MR until I approve. Build and local Azure boot-test are fine to run without asking; code leaving the branch is not.

The problem. On RHEL, azure-cli is still installed via pip onto the system python (old 2.37.0), so it fails Tenable scans. It’s a recurrence: back in STOI-6203 the pip→rpm migration was only done for Ubuntu and SLES — the RHEL path in the shared azure-cli role was silently left on pip, which is why it came back on the Oct RHEL 9.6 image. The research found the RHEL 8/9/10 branches of that role still do pip installs while Ubuntu (apt/MS repo) and SLES (zypper) are already on the repo/rpm path.

The fix direction. Bring RHEL in line with Ubuntu/SLES: install azure-cli from the Microsoft rpm that our mirror already carries, state=present (install, not pinned/not version-locked — John confirmed Tenable just wants it current, so let the repo float unless you find a reason to pin). Drop the old ==2.37.0 pin, and remove the pip remnants (uninstall the pip azure-cli and the /usr/local/bin/az it leaves) so the two don’t coexist and Tenable stops seeing 2.37.0 — order the cleanup before the rpm install. It’s one role, so the fix covers every invocation site at once — which also means the blast radius is every RHEL Azure image, so verify and test carefully.

Also investigate (don’t assume): the research flagged that azure-image-setup.yml appears to invoke the azure-cli role twice — once directly and once via base. Check whether that’s intentional or a leftover, and propose whether to collapse it. Treat it as a proposal to me, not an automatic change.

Hard constraints.

Images must pull only from our mirror, never reach out to Microsoft directly — a lot of our consumers (gov / NS-2) have no internet. The MS repo is already defined and enabled for the RHEL 9 Azure partition and Steve’s sync pulls it. Critically: confirm the rpm AND its entire dependency closure (python3.12 etc.) resolve fully from our mirror. Last time some deps came from codeready-builder, which may not be in the RHEL 9 Azure preset. If the build can’t resolve everything from the mirror, STOP and flag exactly which packages/repos are missing — that’s a question for Steve, not something to fix by reaching outside.
Build hosts are FIPS. azure-cli has a known history of “going funky on FIPS” (possibly an IPv6-related issue too). The rpm bringing its own python3.12 should be a better FIPS posture than pip-on-system-python, but validate it, don’t assume.

Testing (this is the whole point — don’t ship it busted). After the image builds, spin up a test VM from it in the Azure commercial account, CLI-driven, the same way we did for the chrony ticket — point the spin-up at the packer file and the cloud/infra vars so it matches the real cloud config, then tear it all down when done. On the booted VM: confirm FIPS is actually on, run az --version to confirm it’s the rpm (and that az resolves to the rpm path, not /usr/local/bin/az), and run a real command, not just the version check. Watch specifically for FIPS/IPv6 breakage.

InSpec. The current az check is version-agnostic and /usr/bin/az-tolerant, so an rpm migration shouldn’t regress it — confirm that. Optionally propose adding a “must be rpm, not pip” assertion so the next recurrence gets caught by the build instead of by Tenable. If InSpec gets touched, bundle it with the roles MR per our usual rule.

Give me the confirmed map + exact change set + blast radius before you start editing, then go ahead and build and boot-test. Hold everything else for my go.
